Additional Terms: Liquidated Damages for Unauthorised Commercial Use

The following terms supplement the Business Source License above and apply to
any use of the Licensed Work that violates the Additional Use Grant.

1. Nature of Damages

   The Licensor has invested substantial time, research, and intellectual
   effort in developing the Licensed Work, including its classification
   heuristics, adapter protocols, and catalog design. Unauthorised commercial
   use causes harm that is difficult to quantify precisely. The parties
   therefore agree that the following amounts constitute a reasonable
   pre-estimate of damages and not a penalty.

2. Liquidated Damages

   Any person or legal entity that makes commercial use of the Licensed Work
   without a valid commercial licence shall owe the Licensor, upon demand:

   (a) USD 10,000 (ten thousand United States dollars) per production instance
       or deployment unit, per calendar month of unauthorised use, retroactive
       to the date on which commercial use first commenced; AND

   (b) BRL 55,000 (cinquenta e cinco mil reais) per production instance or
       deployment unit, per calendar month of unauthorised use under Brazilian
       law, retroactive to the date on which commercial use first commenced.

   Where both (a) and (b) are asserted in the same proceeding, the Licensor
   shall be entitled to whichever amount is greater, not both simultaneously.

3. Minimum Damages and Costs

   In addition to the amounts above, the infringing party shall be liable for:

   (a) all reasonable legal fees and costs incurred by the Licensor in
       enforcing this License;

   (b) any profits derived by the infringing party from the unauthorised
       commercial use; and

   (c) a minimum of USD 25,000 / BRL 137,500 per enforcement action,
       regardless of the duration of infringing use, as compensation for
       investigation, legal preparation, and reputational harm.

4. Jurisdiction

   Disputes arising under these Additional Terms may be brought before the
   courts of Brazil (Foro Central da Comarca de São Paulo, Estado de São Paulo)
   or any court of competent jurisdiction in the country where the infringing
   party is domiciled or operates its principal business, at the Licensor's
   election.

5. Good Faith Cure

   If an infringing party ceases all unauthorised commercial use within 30
   days of receiving written notice from the Licensor, and pays all accrued
   liquidated damages for the period of infringement, the Licensor may, at
   sole discretion, waive the minimum damages in section 3(c). This cure
   provision may only be exercised once per party.

6. Severability

   If any provision of these Additional Terms is found unenforceable under
   applicable law, the remaining provisions continue in full force.

